# End-of-Turn Auto-Commit (lightweight)

Before your final reply, if you changed files this turn, commit with **turn context** (no `git diff` / `git log`):

```bash
AUTO_COMMIT_USER="what the user asked (≤12 words)" \
AUTO_COMMIT_DONE="what you changed (≤20 words)" \
AUTO_COMMIT_TITLE="type(scope): specific imperative change" \
AUTO_COMMIT_BODY="one line: what changed; why only if needed" \
bash scripts/auto-commit-turn.sh
```

## Title rules (strict)
- `type(scope): verb + specific change` — max 72 chars, no trailing period
- `type`: feat | fix | refactor | docs | chore | test | ci | style | perf
- `scope`: smallest accurate area (`auth`, `watchlist`, `web`, `cursor`, …)
- Must name **what** changed, not "update files" / "misc changes"

## Body rules (strict)
- 1 line required when change is non-obvious; max 2 lines / 240 chars
- State what was committed; add why only if title alone is unclear
- No bullets, markdown, or filler

## Examples
```bash
# good
AUTO_COMMIT_TITLE="feat(watchlist): add shared wishlist sync toggle"
AUTO_COMMIT_BODY="Expose sync control on wishlist settings; defaults to enabled for shared lists."

# bad
AUTO_COMMIT_TITLE="chore: updates"
AUTO_COMMIT_BODY="Made some changes to the app."
```

## Speed
- **Always** set `AUTO_COMMIT_TITLE` (+ `AUTO_COMMIT_BODY` when needed) — instant, uses your turn context
- `AUTO_COMMIT_USER` + `AUTO_COMMIT_DONE` are fallbacks for Ollama/heuristic only
- Clean tree → script no-ops instantly
- Never push unless asked
